# A numeric declaration cannot consume a deferred reference destination.
# Its initializer is not reached when the destination has the wrong type.
# Step 21.7 K2, S3: the record Code is written inside D and launched by `D\Code()` over its data parent(Code) = D.
# Its first line declares `int: p touch()` where D holds the reference place `@: p Target`: the type is checked on
# that line, before the initializer's effects. `touch` records each of its runs in the graph (`Observed\touches`):
# it is run once before the launch as the control of that record (touches 1) and the launch adds none (still 1);
# `reached` stays 0. The launch answers 4.
# The former record `call(D; Code)` still fits the outer form [args, code] with D as the args actual; the
# substitution of DATA is removed (the data is parent(Code)), and D's fields name no input of it: the launch answers 5.
# Norm: the book #structural-call (the data is parent(C)), the roles of data and code (#data-code-roles: «in code it
# checks the type on its own line»).
Observed:
    int: touches 0
end: Observed
fn: touch () int
    Observed\touches := Observed\touches + 1
    return: 7
D:
    Target:
        int: n 30
    end: Target
    @: p Target
    int: reached 0
    Code:
        int: p touch()
        reached := 1
    end: Code
end: D
Main:
    int: control touch()
    D\Code()
